let __v_15 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"carrot\",\"peel or just scrub them, cut into batons, and boil or steam for 6 to 8 minutes until a knife goes in easily. Roast batons at 200C (180C fan) for 25 to 30 minutes with oil and a little honey. Whole small ones take 10 to 12 minutes boiled, and older carrots are sweeter and take longer.\"],[\"cabbage\",\"shred it and cook it briefly, or it goes grey and soft: boil or steam 4 to 5 minutes, or fry it in butter for 5 to 6 with a splash of water and the lid on for the first half. Salt at the end. Red cabbage is the exception and wants a slow braise of 45 minutes or more, with vinegar and something sweet.\"],[\"spinach\",\"it needs seconds, not minutes: wash it, put it in a hot pan with a knob of butter and stir for 1 to 2 minutes until it has just collapsed, then drain off the water it gives. Boiling it makes it slimy; squeeze it dry and dress it with butter, nutmeg or a little garlic.\"],[\"asparagus\",\"snap off the woody end of each spear (it breaks where it should), then boil or steam 3 to 4 minutes for thin spears or 5 to 6 for fat ones, or roast at 200C (180C fan) for 10 to 12 minutes with oil, salt and lemon. It is done when a knife goes in with no crunch left.\"],[\"leek\",\"slice it first, then wash it: grit hides between the layers, so swill the slices in a bowl of water and lift them out. Sweat them in butter with the lid on for 8 to 10 minutes, stirring now and then, until soft but not browned, and a splash of water stops them catching. They go sweet, and they are the base of potato and leek soup.\"],[\"courgette\",\"slice them about 1cm thick, salt them for 10 minutes and pat them dry if you want them firm, then fry in a hot pan for 3 to 4 minutes a side until golden. To roast, toss in oil and bake at 200C (180C fan) for 20 minutes. Don't crowd the pan: they steam rather than brown.\"],[\"kale\",\"strip the leaves off the stalks, then either steam 4 to 5 minutes until tender, or toss with oil and salt and roast at 180C (160C fan) for 12 to 15 minutes until the edges crisp. Raw kale is fine in a salad if you massage it with oil and lemon for a minute first, which softens it.\"],[\"swede\",\"peel and cube it, then boil for 20 to 25 minutes until it mashes easily, and mash it with butter, salt and pepper - a little cream or a knob of butter takes away the sharpness. It roasts well too, in 2cm cubes at 200C (180C fan) for 30 to 35 minutes.\"],[\"globe artichoke\",\"cut the top inch off, snip the points off the outer leaves, and boil or steam 25 to 35 minutes until a leaf near the middle pulls away easily. Eat the leaves dipped in butter or vinaigrette, then scrape out the fuzzy choke and eat the heart. To roast, halve them, oil the cut sides and pack them cut down in a dish at 200C (180C fan) for 25 to 30 minutes with a splash of water and a lid for the first half.\"],[\"jerusalem artichoke\",\"scrub them instead of peeling, since the skin is thin, and roast them 25 to 30 minutes at 200C (180C fan) in oil, or boil 10 to 12 minutes and mash them with butter like potato: they go sweet and nutty fast, so test early. A squeeze of lemon or a splash of vinegar keeps them from discolouring, and start with a small portion, because they earn their other name.\"]]}")?;
