let __v_344 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"courgette\",\"courgettes\",\"zucchini\"]},\"Courgette goes soggy because it is mostly water, so the water has to come out before it hits the pan: slice it about 1cm thick, salt it for 10 to 15 minutes, pat it dry, then cook it in a wide hot pan in one layer with no lid, or roast it at 220C spread out. Crowding the pan steams it, and a lid guarantees it - and if it is going in a salad, salt it and drain it first rather than dressing it early.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cucumber\",\"cucumbers\"]},\"Cucumber goes soggy because salt and dressing pull the water out of it: slice it, salt it for 10 minutes and drain it on kitchen paper, or scoop the watery middle out with a teaspoon, and dress it at the table rather than an hour before. For a sandwich it wants to be patted dry and kept away from the bread until you eat.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"aubergine\",\"aubergines\",\"eggplant\",\"eggplants\"]},\"Aubergine goes soggy when it soaks up oil: salt the slices for 20 minutes, pat them dry, then fry in hot oil in one layer, or brush them with oil and roast at 200C until the edges colour. It is done when it collapses and looks glossy, not when it is merely soft.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mushroom\",\"mushrooms\"]},\"Mushrooms go soggy when they are crowded or covered: they are mostly water, so give them a wide hot pan with a little oil, one layer, no lid, and let the water cook off before you turn them. Salt only at the end, or they give up their water early and stew.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tomato\",\"tomatoes\",\"cherry tomatoes\"]},\"Tomato goes watery when it is cooked with the lid on or with too much salt early: halve them, salt them in a colander for 10 minutes and let the juice run off, then roast them cut side up at 200C, or cook them uncovered so the water reduces instead of pooling.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pepper\",\"peppers\",\"bell pepper\",\"bell peppers\"]},\"Peppers go soft and watery when they are cooked fast in a crowded pan: cut them into strips of the same size and give them a hot wide pan in one layer, or roast them at 200C until the skins blister - then they are sweet rather than wet.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"butternut squash\",\"squash\",\"pumpkin\"]},\"Squash goes soggy when it is steamed rather than roasted: cut it into 3cm chunks, toss in oil, spread it in one layer and roast at 200C for 30 to 35 minutes, turning once. Boiled or covered, it holds on to the water and turns to mush.\"]}]}")?;
